  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Saturday)\nIssued at 134 PM CDT SUN JUL 26 2026\n\nCentral Alabama remains in an unsettled pattern through the\nweekend as the stalled cold front lifts north. We are currently\nmonitoring a weak impulse exiting our area, accompanied by a\nboundary shifting across our northern counties. Additional\nboundary-driven activity expected later today as the northern\nfeature moves south. As we work into the afternoon, the boundary\nin our north will be across the south, while an additional\nboundary may begin to slide into the north from activity\ncurrently in Kentucky. These boundaries will trigger more showers\nand storms. Activity in the north will be limited as the bulk of\nthe instability will be in the central and southern sections of\nthe area. Overall coverage will be isolated to scattered, so\ngenerally only have 20-40% rain chances in the afternoon to\naccount for this, with the highest in the central and south.\nShowers and storms may linger through 9 or 10 PM in the far\nsouth, before dissipating for the night. Cloud cover and rain\nshould keep the temperatures down in the southeast through the\nearly afternoon as well. In the north and west, we will see\ntemperatures in the low to mid 90s, so afternoon heat index\nvalues will climb above 100 and approach the 105 Heat Advisory\ncriteria. While heat index values in the north and west will\napproach the 105 F threshold, current dewpoints are trending\nlower than in previous days. Consequently, confidence is too low\nfor a Heat Advisory, though isolated areas may briefly touch\n105 F this afternoon.\n\nWhile the ridge building will lead to rising temperatures\nbeginning Monday, a mid-level shortwave will bring in a Mesoscale\nConvective System (MCS) from the north. Confidence has begun to\nincrease in the overall placement of the system and timing, so\nprecipitation chances have been raised. Although the system will\nlikely weaken as it approaches our northern counties, a lingering\nrisk for strong to marginally severe storms persists in the\nnorthwest through 10 AM. Winds will be the biggest concern with\nthis system. With this MCS moving through, confidence on\ntemperatures recovering enough to warrant a Heat Advisory on\nMonday remains low.\n\nTuesday is shaping up to be the hottest day of the week, with\nhighs reaching the mid to upper-90s under the influence of the\nridge. The MCS train will continue Tuesday, but at this time it\nappears that the main track will be to our east, so rain chances\nare minimal and the heat most likely will be oppressive. A Heat\nAdvisory has been issued for Tuesday. By mid-week, the ridge\nappears to become slightly suppressed as a trough builds over the\nEast Coast and a deep low forms over New England. This pattern\nchange will allow for slightly cooler temperatures to filter in\nfrom the north, with highs trending toward the upper-80s and low-\n90s by next Friday. Confidence in the timing and intensity of\npotential MCS systems remains limited, resulting in lower\nprobabilities of precipitation for the latter half of the\nworkweek.\n\n16",
